m of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨-x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186424521 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\,3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424522 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\,({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424523 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\,{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186424524 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186425876 "![](images/01/014c2310bcac5b6e9c2be2107bf8a5613b689ff29a8018d2be74fc607dd996db.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨7-2{